ifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068700 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068700 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of Two Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4fafc8c8-0452-582f-b603-6f633de9a39a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of One or No Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "05669abb-838d-5b1c-b97f-833a28b1d413" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Range of a Parameter Given Two, One, or No Intersection Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2180b368-597c-5b99-a9ef-346169711637" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding Intersections of Lines and Reciprocal Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186069850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f7fe334b-af06-545b-8320-d04ce420b4ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068998 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068998 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068998 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068998 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068998 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068998 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068998 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068998 #edb/ref 1045 "Left and Right Continuity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068998 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186069571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068998 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068998 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "acac3ae8-22e4-541d-b0da-b38b7e642d06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069272 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069272 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3471" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069272 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069272 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ intersects the line $4y-5x=8$ at the points $(p,q)$ and $(r,s).$ What is $p+q+r+s?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069272 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9bdc8c8-a628-5a28-9c53-49f974293c50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069272 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we solve for $y$ in the second curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-5x & =8 \\\\ 4y & =5x+8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y=\\frac{5x+8}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{5x+8}{4} \\\\ 4 & =5{x}^{2}+8x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+8x-4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(5x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-2$ and $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{2}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nFinally, if we let $p=-2,q=-\\frac{1}{2},r=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $s=\\frac{5}{2},$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-2-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d995288e573a22db67c7d4c82c1a12bcbb41fd880f706b9fb62062aa90e2d5ba.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069312 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069312 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069312 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069312 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/18/188e0169738a08b3d4580b4f5d3a3ab1aedef87a9d1b83f07c1b10686a38c321.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true in relation to the function $y=g(x)$ above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is continuous at $x=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069312 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f98bb945-e8b3-5349-97c2-0b4b023243ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069312 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x)=0\\ne -2=g(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x)=-2=g(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069334 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069334 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069334 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069334 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069334 #edb/ref 1071 "If the line $y=-2x+3$ and the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1$ intersect at the point $(p,q),$ then $p+q=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069334 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "801b9317-386e-554f-9207-4d232469922d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069334 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+3 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-2}+1 \\\\ -2x+2 & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ (-2x+2)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ 2(-x+1)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ (-x+1)(x-2) & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x+3$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41fee04552a47d93f1dbd788e69e12c4b58e377ec4c0b63c3ed55c712d337f94.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069571 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069571 #edb/ref 1061 12117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069571 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27206838-7dfd-50f0-a4b5-a92905b05017" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069571 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069572 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069572 #edb/ref 1061 12118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069572 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b43b6d85-ec3f-5921-878b-59ca9e5c066f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069572 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069573 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069573 #edb/ref 1061 12119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069573 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "55c60e5f-cbe7-5688-8af0-b8fa5d2c54d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069573 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069574 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069574 #edb/ref 1061 12120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069574 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b88e81b2-e4ca-5ffc-8ade-1a44b0fecea1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069574 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069575 #edb/ref 1061 12121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069575 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1017d5f3-a1be-5979-aa84-1860d0b66f77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069575 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069577 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069577 #edb/ref 1061 12456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069577 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c70c04f3-5555-555a-93b3-8518806961e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069577 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069850 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069850 #edb/ref 1061 466045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069850 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b6e2fa7-06cd-5725-9235-7ae5c5dbd6a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069850 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069851 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069851 #edb/ref 1061 466046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069851 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "674f9cf9-a5de-5045-b6cb-e0e31e295966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069851 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069852 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069852 #edb/ref 1061 466050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069852 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "999d3467-9a05-5d20-9fd5-1b8783ff67e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069852 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069853 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069853 #edb/ref 1061 466051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069853 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "663d62ae-d9e6-5064-8a5b-bec6133beefb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069853 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069854 #edb/ref 1061 466054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069854 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c86492a6-57ef-5081-b122-627fc0b573d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069854 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069959 #edb/ref 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **left-continuous** at the point $x=c$ if the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c^-} f(x) = f(c).\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $(-\\infty,2]$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14b92b0e5a0f482b5e58da975f6f8d369890af97ddc562a4876e41c496f5dfef.png)\n\nNotice that at $x=2,$ the left limit exists, while right limit does not exist because $f(x)$ is not defined for $x>2.$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = 4, \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nBecause the right-sided limit does not exist, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$ However, because the left-sided limit exists and is equal to the function value at $x=2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = 4 = f(2),\n$$\n\nwe can say that the function is **left-continuous** at $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069959 #edb/ref 1065 2596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069959 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "75d31b95-b022-5133-8b0d-54a5ba38c74f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069959 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069960 #edb/ref 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **right-continuous** at the point $x=c$ if the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c^+} f(x) = f(c).\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $[-2,\\infty)$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b625f271c8889466c2a19ed8a333b05e70c2e852058cc1d4469f428d761c799.png)\n\nThe function is **right-continuous** at $x=-2$ because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2^+}f(x) = 4 = f(-2).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069960 #edb/ref 1065 2597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069960 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9424b0a9-43f3-5c35-b8b9-b78b9c69e816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069960 #edb/ref 1067 "Right Continuity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069962 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is said to be **continuous** at a point $x=c$ if \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to c} f(x) = f(c) .\n$$\n\nIn order for the above statement to be true, we require both\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {c}^{-}}f(x)=f(c)\\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to {c}^{+}}f(x)=f(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equalities represent left-continuity and right-continuity. So, we have the following fact:\n\n> A function is **continuous** at a point if and only if it is *both* **left-continuous** *and* **right-continuous** at that point." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069962 #edb/ref 1065 2641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069962 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1dd8ea3c-49f9-5c85-98b3-85b0542e7a8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069962 #edb/ref 1067 "Defining Continuity in Terms of Left-Continuity and Right-Continuity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069975 #edb/ref 1064 "When considering the intersection of a line and a reciprocal function, three possibilities can occur.\n\nThe first possibility is that there are two intersection points, as illustrated in the example below.\n\n![](images/00/00f19933ef5f2e7879012dbd9b87fd86964ed44b74e54749dccd2186e02e2a6a.png)\n\nThe second possibility is that there is only one intersection point. For example:\n\n![](images/aa/aabf0d5ea18c01842890b6e470d7c8eaaf36371565ff7c271ea2ab24ccfc9565.png)\n\nThe final possibility is that there are no intersection points. For example:\n\n![](images/da/da7ecba1887505f162144ab3b86e4cca9be104cda7e78ba8fef3d02f10de2abc.png)\n\nTypically, when computing the points of intersection of a reciprocal function and a line, we have to solve a system of two equations. In the first graph that was shown above, this system was\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system of equations can (usually) be transformed into a single rational equation, which can itself be converted into a quadratic equation. For the above system, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{x} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x^2 = 1.\n$$\n\nBy solving the quadratic equation, we can find the intersection points. And like quadratic equations, there can be two, one, or no real solutions. In this case, there are two solutions:\n\n$$\nx^2 = 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x = \\pm 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069975 #edb/ref 1065 3403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069975 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "70a19ea7-8a66-5b98-b7a2-2312cfe9cf44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069975 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069983 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now turn our attention to the cases where a line and a reciprocal function intersect at either one point or no points.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find where the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}+1$ and the line $y=2$ intersect. To do this, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\ y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and solving for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}+1 & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the corresponding $y$-coordinate, we plug $x=1$ into either equation. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$.\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7da7ea3f7e2444197a7bc402309c22fe23d95483909d3d6674645101f036370.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069983 #edb/ref 1065 4596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069983 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "68835670-e15c-543e-9f1c-ba5dfb7c1823" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069983 #edb/ref 1067 "Intersections of Lines and Reciprocal Functions at One or No Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592